<main id="main">
<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="798" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/mi-claim-calculators/">Calculators</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Claim Deadline Planner</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Free planner</p><h1>Michigan Claim Deadline Planner (with .ics Export)</h1><p class="ph-lede">Missed dates sink more claims than bad estimates. Enter your date of loss and the planner builds a calendar you can download, including a rough estimate of the general Michigan suit-limitation rule with tolling.</p></div></section>
<section class="sec sec-tool"><div class="wrap"><div class="rv">
<div class="tool" data-tool="dates">
<div class="tool-grid">
<fieldset class="tool-in"><legend class="tool-h">Your dates</legend>
<div class="fld"><label for="t-dol">Date of loss</label><div class="inp"><input id="t-dol" name="dol" type="date"></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="t-ntc">Date you reported the claim (optional)</label><div class="inp"><input id="t-ntc" name="ntc" type="date"></div><small class="hint">Used for the tolling estimate below.</small></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="t-den">Date of a formal written denial, if any (optional)</label><div class="inp"><input id="t-den" name="den" type="date"></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="t-rcd">Days your policy allows to claim holdback</label><div class="inp"><input id="t-rcd" name="rcd" inputmode="decimal" value="180"><span class="affix">days</span></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="t-inv">Personal target for the contents inventory</label><div class="inp"><input id="t-inv" name="inv" inputmode="decimal" value="45"><span class="affix">days</span></div></div>
<div class="fld chk"><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="remind" checked> <span>Add a reminder two weeks before each date</span></label></div>
</fieldset>
<div class="tool-out"><p class="tool-h">Your claim calendar</p>
<ol class="cal" data-o="timeline" aria-live="polite"><li><b>Pick your date of loss</b><span>Your milestones will appear here.</span></li></ol>
<button type="button" class="btn btn-blue btn-block" data-ics>Download calendar (.ics)</button>
<p class="hint">The suit-limitation date is a rough planning estimate of the general Michigan rule, not legal advice. Confirm every deadline with a Michigan attorney.</p>
</div></div></div></div>
<p class="hint tool-disc">For planning only. Results depend on the figures you enter and your policy’s terms. They are not a quote, an appraisal or legal advice. Nothing you type is stored or sent.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>What the planner calculates</h2>
<p>The planner adds your policy’s holdback window and a personal inventory target to the date of loss. It also estimates the general Michigan suit-limitation date. Michigan’s Insurance Code generally requires suits under a property policy to be started within one year after the loss, or a longer period if the policy allows, and pauses (tolls) that period from the day you give notice of the loss until the insurer formally denies liability. If you enter a notice date and a denial date, the planner adds that paused time back in.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Not legal advice</b>Deadline rules have exceptions and depend on your exact policy and facts. Use this as a reminder system and confirm every date with a Michigan attorney.</div>
<h2>Using the calendar file</h2>
<p>The .ics download works with Google Calendar, Outlook and Apple Calendar. Each milestone is an all-day event, with an optional reminder two weeks ahead. Nothing you enter leaves your browser.</p>
<p>Next, check how ready your file is with the <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/appraisal-readiness-score/">appraisal readiness score</a>.</p>
<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary><span>What counts as a formal denial?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>That’s a legal question. A partial payment letter may or may not be a formal denial of liability. Ask a Michigan attorney rather than relying on the planner.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Does demanding appraisal stop the suit clock?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Don’t assume it does. Appraisal and the suit-limitation period are separate, so protect your deadline with legal advice while appraisal runs.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Is my data saved?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>No. The planner runs in your browser and the calendar file is created on your device.</p></div></details></div>
